## Create beads and wire dependencies

Create approved epics first, then their children and standalone items. Compose
every description as GitHub-flavored Markdown in a temporary file and use
`--body-file` so formatting is preserved:

```bash
bd create "<epic title>" --type epic --priority <0-4> --labels "<labels>" --body-file <epic-body-file>
bd create "<child title>" --parent <epic-id> --type <type> --priority <0-4> --labels "<labels>" --body-file <child-body-file>
bd create "<standalone title>" --type <type> --priority <0-4> --labels "<labels>" --body-file <item-body-file>
```

Omit `--labels` when no established label applies. Capture every returned bead ID.
Then wire each confirmed prerequisite in the correct direction:

```bash
bd dep add <blocked-id> <blocker-id>
bd dep cycles
```

If creation or dependency wiring fails, stop further mutations, preserve the IDs
already created, and report the exact partial state instead of pretending the
operation was atomic.

## Retrospective coverage and coherence pass

Re-read the approved planning source (the specification or informal-request plan),
not merely your decomposition notes. Re-open every created bead and inspect the
final hierarchy and dependency graph. Build a coverage matrix mapping each source
requirement or approved plan step to bead IDs, then verify:

- every source unit, constraint, edge case, and required cross-cutting concern is covered;
- every ticket is coherent, independently actionable, and free of duplicated scope;
- epic membership is meaningful and every epic has at least two children;
- acceptance criteria and verification are sufficient to establish completion;
- types, priorities, and labels match project conventions and actual impact;
- dependencies point from blocked work to the real blocker, form no cycle, and do
  not serialize work that can proceed in parallel.

Correct the created set before finishing: add missing beads, update inaccurate or
overlapping tickets, reparent misplaced children, adjust priorities/labels, and add
or remove dependency edges as needed. Do not rewrite unrelated pre-existing beads.
Repeat the coverage check until every source unit has one clear implementation home
and `bd dep cycles` reports no cycle.

## Final ticket handoff

Print the final epic → child hierarchy, followed by standalone beads. For every
entry include ID, title, type, priority, and direct blockers. Then report:

- the complete list of bead IDs created in this run;
- any pre-existing bead IDs reused for coverage, separately identified;
- the source-to-bead coverage matrix;
- confirmation that the retrospective found complete coverage, no duplicated
  scope, and an acyclic dependency graph;
- any failures or unresolved questions that prevent the ticket set from fully
  representing the approved work.

Do not claim full coverage while any source unit is unmapped or any creation/wiring
failure remains unresolved. Stop after this handoff; implementation happens in the
ticket-processing workflow, not in this planning conversation.